                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Stuck being kind of mixed on this one. \n\nComplaints: \n\n[*] I don’t think I understood the world building. The wording of most of it is clunky and hard to understand.  For example; that I don’t know what is classed as a “monster” in this world. It could refer to literal monsters, it could include witches. But witches feel like their own classification at the same time. Not helped by non-witch monsters being made by witches often enough to confuse.  \n\n[*] The clunky text is a problem in general. English does not feel like the devs’ primary language and the problem of trying to fit everything into a limited number of text boxes is haunting the writing. By the end I still don’t understand the philosophy of one of the important instigators. \n\n[*] Speaking of, the characters are quite flat. They typically have a quirk or interesting backstory, only one having both. But besides a certain someone, I feel like they needed way more information given. Otherwise, they can be summarised in one sentence without missing anything out.  \n\n[*] Three of the cheevos can be buggy: BAD ENDs, death methods, and the character profiles. I’ve personally not been able to get any of them to register. \n[/list] \n \n\nNeutral: \n\n[*] There are a mix of original and pre-made assets, causing an art style clash you can’t avoid noticing. How much it bothers a person depends on the individual. As a regular of these kinds of game engines, at worst I chuckle and move on. \n\n[*] The optional cooking side objective adds nothing. Extra dialog to flesh out at least Charlotte would have been something.  \n[/list] \n\nPositives: \n\n[*] The puzzles and side quests aren’t brain dead easy. Which is a nice change from the usual. The downside means a lot of going back and forth to find how to progress. \n\n[*] I like the concept of each of the characters. They’re all unique and interesting. I want to get to know them more. \n\n[*] The protagonist Charlotte feels like a good blend of still-childish and having to mature into a world that will be difficult to live in. The way she plays detective while trying to genuinely understand the truths others hide from her is compelling. It’s unsurprising why she’s usually top of the character popularity polls. \n\n[*] I appreciate the extras menu, love when games include them. It includes hints on content you’ve missed which is awesome for people who want to work things out only with in-game methods.  \n[/list] \n \n\nOverall: \n\nI wish we had gotten a more fleshed out version of this game. More dialog (with a beta reader) to help illustrate the world and how the characters are affected by a world that does not like them for who they all are. I streamed the game to some buddies and the fact that none of by the end really understood the lore means we’re all dumb, or the messaging wasn’t written the best.",
